# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in real values. `.env` is gitignored (.gitignore:7).
#
# STANDING RULE (MIGRATION_PLAN.md, P3): every new key is added to this file in the
# SAME COMMIT that introduces it. This file is the only signal the other tracks get
# that a new variable exists. A key added without it produces `undefined` at runtime
# with no indication of what is missing.
#
# Code references below name a symbol as well as a line, because line numbers rot.
# Every one in this file was stale before the TTS work - they still pointed into
# server.ts after the route-module split moved the code into src/server/routes/.

# --- Required ---

# GEMINI_API_KEY: server-side only, never exposed to the browser.
# Read in getGenAI() at src/server/routes/assistant.ts:47,49 and reported
# (presence only) as hasGeminiKey at src/server/routes/health.ts:39 via /api/health.
# Without it, getGenAI() returns null and EVERY /api/assistant/process request silently
# falls through to fallbackProcessVoiceInput (src/server/routes/assistant.ts:232) — the
# keyword matcher.
# Get one at https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
GEMINI_API_KEY=""

# MONGODB_URI: Atlas connection string (Connect -> Drivers -> Node.js).
# Server-side only, read at src/server/db.ts. Never reaches the browser.
# Format: mongodb+srv://<user>:<password>@<cluster>.<hash>.mongodb.net/?retryWrites=true&w=majority
# If the password contains @ : / ? # [ ] or %, it must be percent-encoded.
# A CONNECTION TIMEOUT here means this machine's IP is missing from the Atlas
# Network Access allowlist — not a wrong URI or password. Check that first.
MONGODB_URI=""

# JWT_SECRET: signs access and refresh tokens for the fallback OTP auth path.
# Server-side only, real secret, minimum 32 characters. The server refuses to
# sign or verify without it rather than falling back to a default - a
# predictable signing key is indistinguishable from no authentication at all.
#
# MUST BE BYTE-IDENTICAL ACROSS LOCAL AND DEPLOYED ENVIRONMENTS. If it is not,
# a token issued locally fails verification against the deploy and vice versa,
# and the symptom is a 401 that reads as a bug in the verification code. Two
# people will debug the middleware before either checks the secret. Generate it
# ONCE and distribute it through the agreed P3 channel.
#
# Also the fallback seed for REVIEW_LINK_SECRET and TTS_URL_SECRET, so rotating
# it invalidates every review link. See CLAUDE.md.
#
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
JWT_SECRET=""

# --- Voice output: server-side TTS ---
#
# Punjabi speech is synthesised on the SERVER and cached, not spoken by the
# browser. Where a device has no pa-IN voice - Windows, most Android, all iOS -
# the old browser path transliterated Gurmukhi into Latin and read it with an
# English voice, which a native speaker cannot understand. Punjabi is tonal and
# an English voice has no tone and no retroflex series, so no amount of
# dictionary work fixed it. That is what these keys exist to replace.
#
# None of these carry a VITE_ prefix. They are server-side secrets and must
# never reach the browser bundle.

# SARVAM_API_KEY: tier-1 provider (Sarvam bulbul:v3, speaker "simran").
# Read lazily at first call in src/server/tts/sarvam.ts, never at module load.
# Presence is reported as hasTtsKey by /api/health.
#
# With NEITHER this nor GOOGLE_TTS_API_KEY set, POST /api/tts/resolve returns
# 503 tts_unavailable. Hindi and English then fall back to the browser voice,
# which is adequate for them; Punjabi deliberately stays SILENT and shows the
# on-screen message instead, because unintelligible audio is worse than none.
# Get one at https://dashboard.sarvam.ai
SARVAM_API_KEY=""

# --- Optional (voice output) ---

# GOOGLE_TTS_API_KEY: tier-2 TTS fallback (Google Cloud, pa-IN-Wavenet-A).
# Server-side only. Tried only when Sarvam is unconfigured or fails, so one
# vendor outage does not take voice output down mid-demo.
# Console -> APIs & Services -> Credentials, with the Text-to-Speech API enabled.
# GOOGLE_TTS_API_KEY=""

# TTS_URL_SECRET: keys the PRIVATE audio cache and signs its playback URLs.
# Server-side only, real secret, 32+ characters.
#
# Two uses, derived under different labels (src/server/tts/secret.ts) so the two
# keys stay independent of each other and of the seed:
#
#   - Private cache entries - assistant replies, which carry a worker's name, a
#     customer's name and an amount - are keyed by HMAC rather than by a plain
#     hash of their text. Under a plain hash, anyone who knew the reply template
#     could hash a guessed name/amount tuple and ask whether it returns 200,
#     which is a confirmation oracle over real people. HMAC removes that.
#   - Their playback URLs carry a one-hour signature bound to the owning user.
#     An <audio src> cannot send an Authorization header, which is why
#     GET /api/tts/audio is unauthenticated and the protection lives in the URL.
#     Do not "fix" that route by adding requireAuth: it breaks all playback and
#     the symptom, silence, looks nothing like an auth error.
#
# When unset, a key is DERIVED from JWT_SECRET under a fixed label - the same
# fallback REVIEW_LINK_SECRET uses, so voice does not silently stop working on a
# deploy that has not set this yet. Kept SEPARATE from REVIEW_LINK_SECRET
# because they rotate differently: rotating this invalidates only signatures
# that would expire within the hour anyway, while rotating the review key
# invalidates every review link ever sent.
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
# TTS_URL_SECRET=""

# --- Firebase phone auth (P4) ---
#
# These four are CLIENT-side and are NOT secrets. Firebase web config is
# designed to ship in the browser bundle; access is controlled by Authorized
# Domains and Security Rules, not by hiding the apiKey. That is why they carry
# the VITE_ prefix, which deliberately exposes them to the client bundle.
#
# A Firebase ADMIN service account would be a different thing entirely and a
# real secret - server-side only, no VITE_ prefix. It was once expected to
# arrive alongside requireAuth, but that middleware
# (src/server/middleware/auth.ts:40) verifies the app's own OTP-issued JWTs
# instead. There is no admin SDK in this repo and nothing to set for one.
#
# Console -> Project settings -> General -> Your apps -> SDK setup and configuration.
# Vite reads .env only at startup: restart the dev server after editing these.
# CURRENTLY INERT - commented out deliberately, not by oversight.
#
# Nothing in src/ reads these four, and the firebase SDK is never imported,
# even though the package is a dependency. They were scaffolding for the P4
# phone-auth plan that OTP + JWT ended up covering instead.
#
# Left uncommented they read as required setup, and render.yaml still marks
# them sync: false - so every deploy prompts for four secrets that do nothing.
# Uncomment these AND re-add them to render.yaml if Firebase auth is ever
# actually wired up.
# VITE_FIREBASE_API_KEY=""
# VITE_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=""
# VITE_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=""
# VITE_FIREBASE_APP_ID=""

# --- Optional (client) ---

# VITE_DEMO_TOKEN was deleted when login landed. It was a bearer token for the
# seeded demo worker that shipped inside the browser bundle. The app now signs
# in for real via /api/auth/otp/*, so there is nothing to set here.

# VITE_USE_API: per-screen data source switch (src/lib/featureFlags.ts),
# mapped onto App's three state slices by src/lib/dataSource.ts.
#
# A DATA DOMAIN is API-backed when ANY screen that reads it is. Several screens
# share a slice - home shows all three, passport and profile both read the
# profile - and a slice cannot be half-migrated, because it has one value. The
# consequence worth knowing before Day 9: VITE_USE_API="home" pulls ALL THREE
# domains onto the API, because the dashboard displays all three. It is not a
# smaller step than "all".
#
# When a domain is API-backed its localStorage key stops being written, so that
# no stale shadow copy can quietly become the source of truth again if the flag
# is later turned off.
# Comma-separated list of screens, or "all". Empty/unset means every screen
# reads localStorage, which is the current default and changes nothing.
#   VITE_USE_API="passport"        one screen on the API
#   VITE_USE_API="passport,jobs"   two screens
#   VITE_USE_API="all"             everything
# Vite inlines this at build time - restart the dev server after changing it.
# Day 9 requires a recorded decision on its value in the demo build.
# VITE_USE_API=""

# --- Demo OTP bypass (stage demo only) ---
#
# A fixed OTP for ONE seeded number, so the demo does not depend on venue
# reception or a 90-second SMS delivery window. SERVER-SIDE ONLY: none of these
# carry a VITE_ prefix, so Vite cannot inline them and they cannot reach the
# browser bundle. That prefix is the entire reason VITE_DEMO_TOKEN shipped a
# live credential to every visitor, and this is the same shape of feature.
#
# ALL THREE must be present and well-formed or the bypass is OFF. Absent,
# malformed, or DEMO_OTP_ENABLED set to anything but "true" means off. There is
# no default-on path and no partial config that half-works.
#
# Scoped to DEMO_OTP_PHONE alone. Every other number takes the normal path with
# no behaviour change, and the submitted code is never even examined for them.
#
# DEMO_OTP_CODE is never logged, never returned in a response, and never
# interpolated into an error. Treat it like a password: set it, do not paste it
# into chat, and change it after the event.
#
# DEMO_OTP_ENABLED="false"
# DEMO_OTP_PHONE="+919876543210"
# DEMO_OTP_CODE=""

# Neha's separate, demo-only identifier is 0123456789 (not an SMS number).
# Requires DEMO_OTP_ENABLED=true. Choose a DIFFERENT six-digit code from the worker.
# Never use a VITE_ prefix or commit the actual code.
# DEMO_CUSTOMER_OTP_CODE=""

# --- Optional (server) ---

# REVIEW_LINK_SECRET: signs the HMAC on /r/:token review links. Server-side
# only, real secret, 32+ characters. OPTIONAL.
#
# When unset, a key is DERIVED from JWT_SECRET under a fixed label rather than
# using JWT_SECRET itself, so a leaked review link reveals nothing about the
# token-signing key. Setting a distinct value is better - a review link and a
# session token should not share a compromise - but the fallback exists so that
# review links do not silently stop being issued on a deploy that has not set
# this yet.
#
# Changing it invalidates every review link already sent.
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
# REVIEW_LINK_SECRET=""

# PUBLIC_ORIGIN: absolute origin the public passport QR encodes, e.g.
# "https://kaarigar.onrender.com". Server-side only, no VITE_ prefix.
#
# When unset the origin is reconstructed per-request from x-forwarded-proto and
# the Host header, which is correct on Render and correct locally. Set it
# explicitly before generating any QR that will be PRINTED or screenshotted -
# that is the only way to guarantee the code encodes the domain you demo on
# rather than whatever host answered the request that produced it.
# PUBLIC_ORIGIN=""

# MONGODB_DB_NAME: database inside the cluster. Defaults to "kaarigar".
# The deploy uses kaarigar_sih_demo (render.yaml). See CLAUDE.md before seeding
# against it — never with --reset, which drops jobs including rows with
# outstanding review links.
# MONGODB_DB_NAME="kaarigar"

# PORT: listening port, defaults to 3000. Render/Railway/Fly inject this
# themselves, so it normally stays unset locally and untouched in the platform.
# PORT="3000"

# NODE_ENV: "production" serves the built dist/ (server.ts:38); anything else runs Vite dev middleware.
# NODE_ENV="development"

# DISABLE_HMR: "true" disables hot module reload and file watching (vite.config.ts:25,27).
# DISABLE_HMR="false"

# --- Optional (scripts) ---

# TEST_BASE_URL: target for `npm run test:regressions`
# (scripts/test-regressions.ts:18). Defaults to http://localhost:3000.
# The suite drives the real OTP endpoints and reads devCode from the response,
# so it only works where NODE_ENV is not "production".
# TEST_BASE_URL="http://localhost:3000"

# ===========================================================================
# Booking commitments & reliability
# ===========================================================================
#
# See KAARIGAR_RELIABILITY_FEATURE.md §9. Every window below is optional and
# falls back to the default shown; a malformed value warns to the log and uses
# the default rather than taking the server down at boot.

# --- The master switch ---

# BOOKINGS_ENABLED: "true" turns on booking commitments and the reliability
# ledger. DEFAULT FALSE, and false is byte-for-byte the behaviour that shipped
# before the feature existed: no booking is created, no slot or arrival code is
# required on any transition, no reliability event is written, /api/bookings and
# /api/workers/:handle/stats answer 404, and the trust rubric reads its original
# cancellation-rate source.
#
# LEAVE IT OFF UNTIL THE REHEARSAL PASSES WITH IT ON. Every route it touches is
# one both roles already depend on.
#
# One rule holds in BOTH states: a job with no linked booking - a worker's own
# jobs, and everything seeded or created before the flag was turned on - keeps
# the one-tap path forever. Only customer-linked jobs created while the flag is
# on get a booking.
# BOOKINGS_ENABLED="false"

# --- Required for check-in ---

# CHECKIN_OTP_SECRET: HMAC key for the 4-digit arrival code the customer gives
# the kaarigar on the doorstep. At least 32 characters. Generate with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
#
# ITS OWN SECRET, DELIBERATELY NOT JWT_SECRET. Rotating JWT_SECRET invalidates
# every outstanding review link, so it must not be rotated; hanging the arrival
# codes off it too would make that constraint harder still. Rotating THIS one
# only means open bookings need a re-issued code, which is recoverable.
#
# Server-side only. A VITE_ prefix would inline it into the browser bundle,
# which would hand every visitor the key to forge an arrival.
#
# Until this is set, check-in throws on first use. Everything else still runs.
# CHECKIN_OTP_SECRET=""

# --- Deadlines (minutes unless noted) ---

# ACCEPT_WINDOW_URGENT_MIN: time to make a FIRST RESPONSE - a quote or a
# decline - to an urgent request. Past it the request expires and the job is
# cancelled. Default 30.
# ACCEPT_WINDOW_URGENT_MIN="30"

# ACCEPT_WINDOW_NORMAL_MIN: the same, for a normal request. Default 240.
# ACCEPT_WINDOW_NORMAL_MIN="240"

# SCHEDULE_WINDOW_MIN: time to choose a slot once the CUSTOMER HAS AGREED THE
# PRICE. Armed at that moment and not before, which is why no clock runs while
# the kaarigar is waiting on the customer. Past it the booking expires, the job
# is cancelled, and the kaarigar takes a LATE_CANCEL. Default 1440 (24h).
# SCHEDULE_WINDOW_MIN="1440"

# ARRIVAL_GRACE_MIN: arriveBy = slotEnd + this. Default 60.
# ARRIVAL_GRACE_MIN="60"

# NO_SHOW_AFTER_MIN: how long after arriveBy a LATE booking becomes a NO_SHOW.
# Default 1440 (24h).
# NO_SHOW_AFTER_MIN="1440"

# --- Limits ---

# RESCHEDULE_MIN_NOTICE_H: hours of notice a reschedule needs. Default 12.
# RESCHEDULE_MIN_NOTICE_H="12"

# LATE_CANCEL_NOTICE_H: a kaarigar cancel inside this many hours of slotStart
# counts as a late cancel (-1). Default 12.
# LATE_CANCEL_NOTICE_H="12"

# MAX_RESCHEDULES: reschedule ATTEMPTS per booking - a proposal the customer
# rejected still spends one. 0 turns reschedules off entirely. Default 1.
# MAX_RESCHEDULES="1"

# CHECKIN_OTP_MAX_ATTEMPTS: wrong arrival codes before check-in locks.
# NOT the same setting as the login OTP's attempt cap in auth/tokens.ts.
# Default 5.
# CHECKIN_OTP_MAX_ATTEMPTS="5"

# CHECKIN_LOCK_MIN: how long check-in stays locked after that. Default 15.
# CHECKIN_LOCK_MIN="15"

# MAX_SLOT_HOURS / MAX_SLOT_DAYS_AHEAD: a slot longer than the first, or
# starting further ahead than the second, is refused. Defaults 12 and 14.
# MAX_SLOT_HOURS="12"
# MAX_SLOT_DAYS_AHEAD="14"

# SWEEP_BATCH_SIZE: documents one sweeper pass touches per rule. A full batch
# means there is more to do and the next tick does it. Default 200.
# SWEEP_BATCH_SIZE="200"

# --- Demonstrating the LATE state ---
#
# The honest way to show a no-show on stage without waiting a day. Restart the
# server with these, walk the booking to COMMITTED, wait ~2 minutes, then call
# the sweeper. Say plainly that the windows were shortened - the mechanism is
# real, only the clock is impatient.
#
#   ARRIVAL_GRACE_MIN="1"
#   NO_SHOW_AFTER_MIN="1"
#   ACCEPT_WINDOW_NORMAL_MIN="2"
#   SCHEDULE_WINDOW_MIN="2"
#
# PUT THESE BACK AFTERWARDS. Left in place on the deployed instance they would
# expire every real request within two minutes of it being made.
